/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  create_terrain DIRT2
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    land_percent 15
    number_of_clumps 6
  }

  create_terrain SNOW
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    land_percent 15
    number_of_clumps 6
  }

  create_terrain DESERT
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    land_percent 15
    number_of_clumps 6
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 2
    spacing_to_specific_terrain SNOW 10
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_78 - when a create_terrain command declares BOTH
spacing_to_other_terrain_types AND spacing_to_specific_terrain against one
of the terrains that generic rule already covers, are the two enforced as
independent minimums, or does one silently override the other?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-016 (the RMSTEST_66/70-74 investigation
noted, without settling it, that Venn.rms combines the two attributes on
the same command and that RMSTEST_31 - the only existing measurement of
spacing_to_other_terrain_types - never exercised that combination),
docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.4, src/preview/generator/terrains.ts
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
RMSTEST_31 measured spacing_to_other_terrain_types alone (against cliff
units, not another terrain) and confirmed it as a plain minimum distance.
It never ran alongside spacing_to_specific_terrain on the same command.
Venn.rms does exactly that, repeatedly (e.g. line ~912: "spacing_to_
other_terrain_types 2 spacing_to_specific_terrain LAYER_FOREST_SPARSE 8"),
and nothing has confirmed the two compose the obvious way - a generic
floor against every foreign terrain, tightened to a larger, terrain-
specific floor against the one named. This is the same SHAPE of question
as the base_terrain/base_layer conjunction bug (Sec.6.4, closed
2026-08-11): two guide-documented spacing rules on one command, and no
guarantee the engine treats them as simply composing rather than one
silently winning.

METHOD
Three terrains on one map. DIRT2 and SNOW are generated first with no
spacing rules of their own, so they compete freely for space and end up
genuinely adjacent to each other in places. DESERT generates last, with
BOTH attributes: spacing_to_other_terrain_types 2 (a loose generic floor,
satisfied by hugging DIRT2) and spacing_to_specific_terrain SNOW 10 (a
much tighter, SNOW-specific floor). If both are enforced independently,
DESERT should come to rest close against DIRT2 (within ~2 tiles) while
staying at least ~10 tiles from every SNOW tile.

PREDICTIONS, AND WHAT EACH ONE LOOKS LIKE IN THE PROBE OUTPUT
  BOTH ENFORCED INDEPENDENTLY (documented reading) -> min distance
    DESERT-to-DIRT2 is small (around 2, RMSTEST_31-style "at or above the
    declared value"); min distance DESERT-to-SNOW is large (around 10,
    clearly bigger than the DIRT2 figure).
  spacing_to_specific_terrain IS IGNORED WHEN THE GENERIC RULE IS ALSO
    PRESENT -> both minimum distances come out around 2 - DESERT sits as
    close to SNOW as it does to DIRT2, ignoring the tighter 10-tile rule
    entirely.
  spacing_to_other_terrain_types IS IGNORED WHEN A SPECIFIC OVERRIDE NAMES
    ONE OF THE SAME TERRAINS -> DESERT keeps ~10 tiles from SNOW but sits
    with NO enforced minimum from DIRT2 at all (distance 0 or 1, patches
    touching), since the generic rule silently drops out once a specific
    one exists.
  THE LARGER VALUE APPLIES TO EVERY FOREIGN TERRAIN -> both minimum
    distances come out around 10.
  THE SMALLER VALUE APPLIES TO EVERY FOREIGN TERRAIN -> both minimum
    distances come out around 2, but with SNOW's occasional accidental
    near-misses at DIRT2-like distances rather than a clean, unenforced
    read the way "specific ignored" would look.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. THREE RUNS.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT2
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DESERT
  For every DESERT patch, compute the minimum tile-to-tile distance to the
  nearest DIRT2 patch and, separately, to the nearest SNOW patch - patch
  bounding boxes are a fast first pass, and if any two overlap the answer
  for that pair is already 0/touching (CLAUDE.md's own bbox-vs-true-
  distance rule from the BUG-016 correction applies here too: if the
  bounding boxes merely CROSS without the patches actually touching,
  re-check with the true minimum before concluding anything). Two clearly
  different numbers (small against DIRT2, large against SNOW) is the
  independent-minimums reading; anything else, read against the four
  alternative predictions above.
================================================================================
*/
